  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "A sample of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>mg</mi></math> of an unknown monohydric alcohol, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>OH</mi></math> was added to methylmagnesium iodide. A gas is evolved and is collected and its volume measured to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math>. The molecular weight of the unknown alcohol is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math>.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "33",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Alcohols on reaction with Grignard reagent forms hydrocarbons.This is due to the presence of active hydrogen in alcohols and here grignard reagent acts as a base.</p><p>When any alcohol is treated with methyl magnemsium iodide the hydrocarbon formed should be methane gas.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ROH</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>MgI</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi>ROMgI</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></math></p><p>Number of moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo></math> Number of moles of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ROH</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Volume</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>Litres</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>the</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gas</mi></mrow><mn>22400</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>the</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gas</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>the</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gas</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> (Assuming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NTP</mi></math> Condition)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>22400</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>Molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>the</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gas</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>MM</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>33</mn></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>mol</mi></math></p></blockquote>",
